WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
Safe file handling is a roadmap, not a single library call.

2
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.646
Start by treating paths as input.

3
00:00:06.646 --> 00:00:10.946
Define allowed locations for reading, writing, temporary files, and output replacement.

4
00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:15.246
Centralize that policy so callers do not invent local string checks.

5
00:00:15.246 --> 00:00:19.937
Validate paths and file contents separately because they answer different safety questions.

6
00:00:19.937 --> 00:00:23.847
Open files with the narrowest mode that fits the operation.

7
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:29.320
Read defensively with size limits, streaming when appropriate, and clear text or binary expectations.

8
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.402
Parse structure before trusting meaning, and pass only validated data into business logic.

9
00:00:34.402 --> 00:00:41.047
When writing, plan for partial failure, intentional truncation, careful permissions, and late errors during flush or close.

10
00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:47.302
Temporary files should be created with race-resistant patterns and cleaned up through a clear lifecycle.

11
00:00:47.302 --> 00:00:53.166
Symlinks, metadata, permissions, and platform differences should be documented instead of treated as invisible details.

12
00:00:53.166 --> 00:00:59.030
RAII should carry routine cleanup, while error handling preserves invariants and avoids leaking sensitive information.

13
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.985
Finally, make file safety reviewable.

14
00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:02.158
Log with restraint.

15
00:01:02.158 --> 00:01:05.676
Test missing, malformed, large, restricted, and platform-specific cases.

16
00:01:05.676 --> 00:01:08.412
Ask who controls each path and file.

17
00:01:08.412 --> 00:01:10.367
Ask what happens on failure.

18
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:15.840
Good file handling is durable because its assumptions are visible, narrow, and continuously checked.
